    "title": {
      "en": "Rational and irrational numbers on the number line"
    },
    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "The diagonal that is not a fraction"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "Draw a square of side 1 and measure its diagonal. By Pythagoras it is the square root of 2, a number that cannot be written as a fraction. Ancient mathematicians found this troubling!"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "rational p/q",
          "irrational",
          "real numbers",
          "infinite between",
          "average",
          "root 2 construction",
          "repeating decimals"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "A rational number can be written as p/q with p, q integers and q not zero; its decimal form terminates or repeats.\nAn irrational number cannot be written as p/q; its decimal form neither terminates nor repeats, like root 2 = 1.41421356...\nReal numbers are the rational and irrational numbers together, and each corresponds to a point on the number line.\nBetween any two real numbers there are infinitely many rational and irrational numbers.\nTo find a rational number between a and b, take their average (a + b)/2.\nTo place root 2 on the number line, draw a right triangle with legs 1 and 1 at 0 and 1; the hypotenuse has length root 2 and an arc with centre 0 marks it.\nA repeating decimal such as 0.333... is rational: 0.333... = 1/3."
        }
      },
